  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "After breaking up with Elena Pavlovna, Chef Barinov wakes up hungover in Max's apartment. While Lyova and Ilya hurry out the door, the Chef recruits Max to go find a morning drink.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Что я здесь делаю?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "lyova",
      "t": "Максим знает, это его комната.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Здравствуйте, шеф, как дела?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Как я здесь оказался?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Вместе уже и выпить нечего!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "lyova",
      "t": "Так, всё, я уже пошёл.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Где все? Пойдёшь со мной.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Найдём выпить что-нибудь сейчас.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У нас дома ничего нет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Собирайся, пойдём вместе опохмелиться.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Шеф, я же на работу опоздаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Меня предупреди, я разрешу тебе опоздать.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Что я здесь делаю?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What am I doing here?' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'what' before landing on 'do'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lyova",
      "t": "Максим знает, это его комната.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Maxim knows, this is his room.' The grammar packs 'knows' and 'room' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Здравствуйте, шеф, как дела?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hello, Chef, how are things?' Max raises the pitch on 'things to do' to turn the phrase into a direct spoken question. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Как я здесь оказался?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'How did I end up here?' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'how' before landing on 'turned out to be'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Вместе уже и выпить нечего!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'There is nothing left to drink together!' In Russian, the idea of 'together' flows naturally into 'nothing' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lyova",
      "t": "Так, всё, я уже пошёл.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Alright, that is it, I am leaving now.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'so' and finishing on 'went'. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Где все? Пойдёшь со мной.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Where is everyone? You will come with me.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'where' before landing on 'me'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Найдём выпить что-нибудь сейчас.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We will find something to drink now.' Chef Barinov brings out the contrast between 'will find' and 'to drink' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У нас дома ничего нет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We have nothing at home.' Notice how Russian expresses 'at' and 'no' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Собирайся, пойдём вместе опохмелиться.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Get ready, let us go cure this hangover together.' In Russian, the idea of 'gather yourself' flows naturally into 'to cure a hangover' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Шеф, я же на работу опоздаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Chef, I am going to be late for work.' The meaning hinges on 'Chef Barinov' working together with 'will be late' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Меня предупреди, я разрешу тебе опоздать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Just warn me, and I will allow you to be late.' Chef Barinov addresses the listener with the informal singular 'you' alongside 'to be late'. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Что я здесь делаю?",
      "gap": 0.5
